    "body": "## What this is\n\nA **spike**, not a merge candidate. It answers one question: is Hazel's\nevaluator meaningfully faster compiled by `wasm_of_ocaml` than by\n`js_of_ocaml`? The evaluator is compute-bound and already runs off the UI\nthread in a Web Worker, which makes it the part of Hazel most likely to\nbenefit from Wasm GC \u2014 boundary-crossing costs, which is where Wasm usually\nloses, barely apply there.\n\n## The constraint that shaped the whole branch\n\n`virtual_dom` v0.16 requires `js_of_ocaml < 6.0.0`. `wasm_of_ocaml` is only\nreleased from 6.0.1 onward. **They cannot coexist in one opam switch.**\n\nEverything else here follows from that:\n\n- the benchmark links only `language` + `menhirParser`, never `haz3lcore`\n  or `web`;\n- parsing goes through `menhirParser` rather than the zipper path the CLI\n  uses, since `PersistentZipper`/`MakeTerm` live in `haz3lcore`;\n- `language` had to be made Bonsai-free, which is what the `util` split and\n  the livelit removal are for.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Split `util`** into `util` (pure + `js_of_ocaml` only) and `util_web`\n  (Bonsai/Virtual_dom). `Util_web` re-exports all of `Util`, so most\n  consumers only changed `open Util` to `open Util_web`. `JsUtil` and `Key`\n  were each *split* rather than moved, keeping their `js_of_ocaml`-only\n  majority in `util` \u2014 that alone avoided ~48 files of churn.\n- **Dropped livelits from the language core.** The three builtin livelits\n  construct vdom directly and were the last thing holding `Virtual_dom`.\n  `LivelitCtx` keeps its types so `Ctx.LivelitEntry` still typechecks, but\n  nothing populates it and `LivelitProj` renders a placeholder.\n- **Added `bench/wasm/`**: the benchmark, a switch-setup script, and a\n  runner that builds both backends from the same compiler version.\n- Bumped `(lang dune 3.16)` to `3.17` for `(modes wasm)`.\n\nResulting dependency closure is\n`language \u2192 util \u2192 {ptmap, bignum, uuidm, unionFind, csv}` \u2014 verifiably\nBonsai-free.\n\n## What it gives up\n\nThe livelit deletion is the part that must not merge. The principled version\nregisters livelit views from the web layer rather than deleting them; the\n`util` split, by contrast, is worth keeping on its own merits and is the\nsame prerequisite an Internet Computer port would need.\n\nTwo livelit tests were removed (`^slider(50)` elaboration, `^emotion(50)`\nstatics-map ids) because they assert behaviour the builtins provided. The\n`^^slider` tests exercise the projector, a separate mechanism, and are\nuntouched.\n\n## Verification\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `dune build @check` (dev) | clean |\n| `dune build src` (release, `-warn-error +A`) | clean |\n| `@test-quick` | 3457 tests, 0 failures |\n| benchmark runs end-to-end on node 22 | yes |\n\n## Numbers\n\nNot yet \u2014 the comparison needs the second opam switch, which is still being\nbuilt. A `js_of_ocaml` 5.9.1 smoke run gives 607 ms plain / 596 ms incr on\n`bench/eval-bench.hz`, but that is *not* the baseline: `run.sh` rebuilds\nboth backends from the same 6.x compiler so the delta is attributable to the\nbackend rather than the toolchain. Results to follow in a comment.\n\nCaveat worth stating up front: `eval-bench.hz` is a single workload. Under\n`wasm_of_ocaml` the OCaml heap is managed by the browser's collector rather\nthan OCaml's, so allocation-heavy and allocation-light programs may not move\nin the same direction \u2014 one number is a signal, not a characterization.\n\n## Running it\n\n```\nbench/wasm/setup-switch.sh\ngit worktree add --detach ../hazel-wasm-bench wasm-eval-bench-spike\ncd ../hazel-wasm-bench && bench/wasm/run.sh hazel-wasm 20\n```\n\nNode 22+ is required; Wasm GC postdates the node 18 this repo otherwise\nbuilds against.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
